window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-57acf27f31f7df11"]={"key":"ny_b1-57acf27f31f7df11","group":"ny_b1","uid":"57acf27f31f7df11","rank":54,"title":"bellwether medication management design","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are Nurse Practitioner Cordelia Nakamura supporting Ivo Trzaska on the Bellwether Ridge Behavioral Hospital 24-bed adult inpatient unit, which opens this spring. The unit has no on-site pharmacist, Alderwick Solutions will deliver medications twice daily, and the buildout is close enough that the medication-management design must be settled for review by the Cavendish Authority. Ivo has already told the project group that patients will not be delayed for a first dose or a needed PRN, and that the unit will not depend on a large locked medication room; those assurances are now sitting uneasily beside the hospital's diversion concerns and the ligature-risk review. Use the attached Cavendish Authority medication-governance standard, `cavendish_medication_governance_standard.pdf`, as the governing source and prepare `bellwether_medication_management_design.docx` in the top level of the working directory. Produce one specified end-to-end medication-management design for the unit, written as an implementation briefing for Ivo and the delivery team rather than as a clinical essay. It must explain how orders become available to the right patient, how first doses and PRNs are handled when the next pharmacy delivery or pharmacist review is not yet available, how controlled and refrigerated medicines are secured and reconciled, how discrepancies, refusals, omissions, recalls, and urgent changes are escalated, and what records and handoffs make the design auditable. Address the physical plant and the twice-daily delivery model explicitly, identify the alternatives you ruled out and why they fail here, and make the design usable by a new nurse or covering prescriber without a question to the author. Include a short teaching section that actively discourages the tempting practice of borrowing, sharing, or carrying forward another patient's medication or using emergency stock for convenience. Keep the document to no more than six pages, use plain language with defined responsibilities, and do not invent medication names, patient identifiers, drug quantities, or claims that the attached standard does not support. End with the chosen design and its rationale, not a list of unresolved next steps.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/cavendish_medication_governance_standard.pdf` — Governing source for Cavendish Authority medication storage, emergency supply, pharmacist review, controlled-drug reconciliation, documentation, escalation, and behavioral-health safety requirements.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `bellwether_medication_management_design.docx` — A single instructional implementation briefing specifying and defending the medication-management design for Bellwether Ridge Behavioral Hospital's new 24-bed adult inpatient unit. (Must be written as one coherent design, not a menu of equally recommended options.; Must be no more than six pages when rendered with ordinary document settings.; Must address clinical workflow, physical plant, pharmacy delivery timing, access safeguards, documentation, escalation, and implementation responsibilities.; Must include the alternatives ruled out and the reason each is unsuitable for this unit.; Must include an explicit teaching section discouraging borrowing, sharing, carrying forward, or convenience use of another patient's or emergency medication.; Must not invent medication names, patient identifiers, quantities, or unsupported claims about the governing standard.; Must end with the selected design and its rationale rather than unresolved next steps.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/cavendish_medication_governance_standard.pdf","bytes":4118,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/3f87e73613fc6e8903e742e820c1b12df6e46a9312ce69256db0ffba441e4405.pdf","sha256":"3f87e73613fc6e8903e742e820c1b12df6e46a9312ce69256db0ffba441e4405","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"                         Cavendish Authority\n       Medication Governance Standard — Adult Behavioral-Health\n                           Inpatient Units\n                                  CA-MG-14 | Issued 15 January 2026 | Applicable to inpatient services\n\n  1. Purpose and application\n  1.1 This standard establishes the minimum medication-governance controls for adult behavioral-health inpatient units. Services\n  must apply the controls with their physical-plant safety assessment and local emergency arrangements.\n  1.2 Medication information and records must be available only to people involved in care, operations, safety, or authorized\n  oversight. Handoffs must use the minimum information needed for the receiving role.\n\n  2. Storage and access\n  2.1 Medication must be secured from patients and unauthorized staff. Access is limited to authorized personnel and must be\n  accountable through an access record or equivalent auditable control.\n  2.2 Storage and access hardware must account for ligature risk and use tamper-resistant physical design. A service must not\n  depend on wall-mounted medication cabinets where its physical-plant assessment excludes them.\n  2.3 Refrigerated medication must be held in access-restricted storage with temperature monitoring. A temperature excursion\n  requires corrective action, assessment of product integrity, and a record.\n\n  3. Orders, review, and patient-specific supply\n  3.1 Administration requires a valid clinical authorization and verification of the intended patient, the current order, and relevant\n  safety information. Patient-specific medication must be labeled for that patient and verified before administration.\n  3.2 A pharmacist must review medication orders within the service review window. If waiting for the next delivery or pharmacist\n  review would create an unsafe clinical delay, a clinically authorized emergency or first-dose pathway may be used. The use and\n  reason must be documented and pharmacist review obtained retrospectively.\n  3.3 A patient’s medication must not be used for a different patient. Patient-specific supply must not be relabeled, shared, or\n  carried forward between patients.\n\n  4. Contracted pharmacy delivery and contingency supply\n  4.1 Contracted-pharmacy deliveries require a documented receiving handoff. Authorized receiving staff must verify delivery\n  contents against expected patient-specific and contingency items, place them securely, and record and escalate discrepancies.\n  4.2 Emergency or override stock is for a defined urgent clinical need only. It is not routine convenience stock or a substitute for\n  ordinary supply. Each access must be authorized, documented, inventoried, and reconciled after use.\n\n  5. Controlled medication\n  5.1 Controlled medication requires stronger custody than ordinary medication, including secure restricted access, shift or access\n  reconciliation, and a custody record.\n  5.2 A controlled-medication discrepancy requires prompt escalation, witnessed handling as applicable, investigation, and a record\n  of the discrepancy and resolution.\n\n  6. Administration, exceptions, and transition\n  6.1 The administration record must document administrations, refusals, omissions, PRN indication and response, and urgent or\n  emergency access.\n  6.2 Medication changes, discontinuations, returns or wastage, and unresolved issues must be documented and communicated at\n  handoff. Supply that is no longer active must be reconciled and handled through the authorized return or wastage process.\n  6.3 A service must have an escalation pathway for discrepancies, delayed or unavailable doses, adverse reactions, and urgent\n  medication changes. Immediate patient safety action, appropriate clinical notification, documentation, and follow-up are required.\n\n  Authority note: This standard establishes governance controls; it does not replace a prescriber’s clinical judgment or service\n  emergency procedures.\n\n\n\n\nCavendish Authority | Medication Governance Standard                                                                                Page 1\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/bellwether_medication_management_design.docx","bytes":43349,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/0f77da6a1d1850b6ff0e32af39a7b158daf8c2d19925acf8b79e79df5a188d2d.docx","sha256":"0f77da6a1d1850b6ff0e32af39a7b158daf8c2d19925acf8b79e79df5a188d2d","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Medication-Management Design\nBellwether Ridge Behavioral Hospital | 24-bed adult inpatient unit\nPrepared by Cordelia Nakamura, NP  •  For Ivo Trzaska and the delivery team  •  Opening implementation\nRECOMMENDATION — Centralized secure medication service point with a tightly bounded contingency supply.\nUse a small staff-only, access-controlled medication alcove at the nursing core—not a large locked medication room—with tamper-resistant, non-wall-mounted storage, a restricted refrigerator, controlled-medication custody, and auditable access. Alderwick Solutions supplies patient-specific medicines twice daily; emergency/override access bridges only a defined urgent need.\n1. Operating model and physical plant\nThe medication alcove is behind the staff-only boundary and within normal staff observation, away from patient-accessible hardware. It contains a secured, free-standing/tamper-resistant medication unit with separate locked compartments for patient-specific supply, contingency supply, and controlled medication; a separately locked, temperature-monitored refrigerator; a receiving surface; and a confidential workstation. No wall-mounted medication cabinet is used. Patients do not enter the alcove; authorized nurses, authorized prescribers acting within service policy, and designated authorized pharmacy/receiving personnel have role-based access. Each entry or access is recorded, and unauthorized access is reported immediately to the charge nurse and service lead for safety action and investigation.\nThis is deliberately small: routine medication is patient-specific and replenished through Alderwick’s twice-daily delivery, so the unit does not need a broad ward stock room. The contingency compartment is physically and administratively separate from patient-specific supply. The charge nurse owns access rosters, shift readiness, and escalation; the prescriber owns clinical authorization and urgency decisions; Alderwick pharmacist services own dispensing, labeling, clinical review within the applicable review window, and retrospective review when emergency access is used.\n2. From order to the intended patient\nHandoff\nRequired action\nOwner / record\n1. Order and screen\nPrescriber enters or communicates a valid order through the approved record. Nurse confirms the intended patient, current order, allergies and relevant clinical checks; resolves uncertainty with the prescriber before proceeding.\nPrescriber; administering nurse. Order and clinical record.\n2. Pharmacy process\nOrder is sent to Alderwick for pharmacist review and patient-specific dispensing/labeling. The label and supplied item remain linked to that patient; no relabeling or substitution from another patient.\nAlderwick pharmacist/service. Pharmacy and order records.\n3. Twice-daily receipt\nAt each scheduled delivery, authorized receiver checks the sealed handoff and expected patient-specific and contingency items, records receipt, places items in the correct secure compartment, and immediately logs/escalates missing, unexpected, damaged, or mismatched items.\nDesignated receiving nurse; charge nurse. Delivery handoff and discrepancy record.\n4. Administration\nAuthorized nurse removes only the intended patient’s item, matches patient identity, medication and label to the current order, route and timing, completes relevant safety checks, administers, and records it at once. PRNs also record indication and response.\nAdministering nurse. Administration record.\n\n3. Do not delay a necessary first dose or PRN\nThe promise of timely treatment is met through a controlled exception, not through unsecured stock. When the next Alderwick delivery or pharmacist review is not yet available and waiting would create an unsafe clinical delay, the prescriber gives clinical authorization and the nurse verifies patient identity, the valid order, allergies and relevant safety information. The charge nurse or delegated authorized nurse opens the contingency/override compartment only for that defined urgent clinical need, removes the authorized item for the intended patient, and records the reason, authorization, patient match, item, time, administrator, and any required response in the approved records. The nurse then administers and records the dose; the order and exception are sent for retrospective pharmacist review. A later review is required—it does not replace clinical authorization or permit bypassing verification.\nFor an urgent PRN, the same pathway additionally confirms that the PRN order is current and its indication applies now; the nurse records the indication and clinical response. If an order is absent, unclear, or does not authorize the proposed use, the nurse does not use contingency supply and contacts the prescriber. After every override access, the charge nurse reconciles the compartment and initiates replenishment through Alderwick. Contingency supply is not a standing convenience substitute for the twice-daily process.\n4. Custody, cold chain, changes, and delivery exceptions\nArea\nControl that the oncoming nurse can use\nControlled medication\nKeep in the dedicated restricted compartment with a custody record. Reconcile at each shift and after access; use authorized witnessing where applicable. Any count/custody discrepancy: secure the area and affected supply, protect patient care, notify the charge nurse and service lead promptly, obtain witnessed handling as applicable, document facts, and support investigation and resolution.\nRefrigerated medication\nKeep only in the access-restricted medication refrigerator. Review and record temperature monitoring. On an excursion, restrict use of affected supply, notify the charge nurse and Alderwick for product-integrity direction, take corrective action, and record the event and disposition.\nChanged, stopped, transferred\nPrescriber change is documented in the current order record and communicated promptly to nursing and Alderwick. Nurse removes obsolete patient-specific supply from active use, reconciles it against the record, and uses the authorized return/wastage route; receiving/covering nurse sees the status at handoff.\nRecall, damage, expiry, or delivery mismatch\nQuarantine the item from administration, preserve its identity/custody, notify charge nurse and Alderwick, document the issue, determine affected patients through the record, and complete authorized return or replacement. Do not silently replace it from another patient’s supply.\n\n5. Exceptions and escalation\nEvent\nImmediate response and closure\nRefusal, omission, delay, or unavailable dose\nAssess the patient and clinical significance; do not automatically reissue or mark the dose complete. Record what occurred and why, notify the prescriber when clinical assessment or a replacement plan is needed, notify Alderwick for supply issues, and carry the item at handoff until resolved.\nUrgent regimen change\nPrescriber documents the change; nurse stops/holds prior supply from active use as appropriate and communicates the current status to charge nurse, Alderwick, and the next nurse/covering prescriber. Reconcile physical supply and the administration record.\nAdverse reaction or medication-related emergency\nTake immediate patient-safety action under the service emergency response, summon appropriate clinical assistance, notify the prescriber/clinical lead, preserve relevant medication information, document the event and actions, notify pharmacy as relevant, and ensure follow-up is handed over.\nAny discrepancy or suspected diversion\nSecure medication and records, protect patient care, promptly notify charge nurse and service lead, make the required discrepancy record, use witnesses as applicable, preserve the access/custody trail, and support investigation. Do not normalize, conceal, or “fix” a discrepancy by borrowing stock.\n\n6. Shift change, coverage, and audit trail\nThe outgoing charge or delegated nurse leads a confidential, role-limited medication handoff with the oncoming nurse. It covers: pending or unreviewed orders; expected Alderwick deliveries and supply gaps; first-dose/override use awaiting retrospective pharmacist review; recent PRNs with indication and response; controlled-medication reconciliation; refrigerator excursions; refusals, omissions, delays, urgent changes, returns/wastage, recalls, and every unresolved discrepancy. The oncoming nurse accepts a named owner and due action. A covering prescriber receives only the clinically necessary patient-specific status through the approved record and handoff—not a broad medication list for convenience.\nThe auditable record set is: current order and pharmacist-review record; patient-specific label/dispensing record; delivery receiving and discrepancy log; access record; administration record (including refusals, omissions, PRN indication/response); emergency/override authorization-and-use record; controlled-medication custody and reconciliation record; temperature-monitoring and excursion record; return/wastage and recall/quarantine record; incident/investigation record; and shift-handoff record. Keep records in the approved system and disclose them only to roles involved in care, operations, safety, or authorized oversight.\n7. Teaching point: protect the patient and the trail\nNever borrow, share, relabel, carry forward, or administer another patient’s medication.\nNever use emergency or override supply merely to avoid a pharmacy request, a delivery wait that is not clinically unsafe, or ordinary supply work. These shortcuts break patient-specific verification, obscure custody and inventory, and increase diversion risk. If the needed item is not patient-specific and the urgency threshold is not met, pause and call the charge nurse/prescriber and Alderwick rather than improvising.\n8. Alternatives ruled out\nAlternative\nWhy it fails at Bellwether Ridge\nLarge broadly stocked unit medication room\nIt conflicts with the stated direction not to depend on a large locked medication room; it expands patient-access and diversion exposure and makes a patient-specific, twice-daily delivery model less disciplined.\nWall-mounted cabinet or automated-cabinet model\nThe physical-plant ligature-risk assessment excludes dependence on wall-mounted cabinets. Adding one would defeat the behavioral-health safety constraint rather than resolve it.\nWait for every delivery or pharmacist review\nThis would leave a clinically necessary first dose or PRN unavailable when waiting would create an unsafe delay. It fails the timely-care assurance and the standard’s emergency/first-dose pathway.\nLarge unrestricted override stock\nIt invites convenience use, weakens patient-specific accountability, and heightens diversion risk. It is inconsistent with an exceptional, authorized, documented, inventoried, and reconciled emergency supply.\n\nChosen design and rationale\nBellwether Ridge should implement the centralized secure medication service point with patient-specific twice-daily Alderwick supply and a small, separately controlled contingency pathway. It fits the ligature-risk build by avoiding wall-mounted cabinets and a broad ward stock room, preserves prompt first-dose and PRN care through documented clinical exception, and keeps diversion control credible through restricted auditable access, controlled-drug reconciliation, and post-use inventory review. This is the one design that resolves both Ivo’s no-delay assurance and the Authority’s medication-governance safeguards.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"r01","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies one clear medication-management design as the recommendation for the unit.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"A clearly named or otherwise unmistakably designated recommended model appears in the document.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the document makes one selection rather than presenting several co-equal designs."},{"rubric_item_id":"r02","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The selected design is justified against both the ligature-risk physical plant and the twice-daily contracted-pharmacy delivery model.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The rationale connects design choices to both stated constraints.","grader_hint":"Award only when both constraints materially influence the recommendation, not when they are merely mentioned."},{"rubric_item_id":"r03","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document describes a complete path from a valid medication order to medication availability for the intended patient.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The workflow covers order receipt or verification, pharmacy processing, delivery or authorized access, and patient-specific administration.","grader_hint":"Look for an end-to-end chain rather than isolated storage or administration advice."},{"rubric_item_id":"r04","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design distinguishes patient-specific medication from general emergency or contingency supply.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The document explains the permitted purpose and boundaries of each category.","grader_hint":"The distinction must affect workflow or controls, not just terminology."},{"rubric_item_id":"r05","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design provides a safe response for a clinically necessary first dose when the next delivery or pharmacist review is unavailable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"A timely first-dose pathway is specified with appropriate authorization, verification, documentation, and later review.","grader_hint":"Check that urgency does not eliminate prescribing, identity, allergy, interaction, or documentation safeguards."},{"rubric_item_id":"r06","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design provides a safe response for a clinically necessary PRN when ordinary supply is not immediately available.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The PRN pathway includes order validity, patient matching, administration record, and limits on contingency access.","grader_hint":"Do not award for a generic statement that PRNs are available."},{"rubric_item_id":"r07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document defines who may authorize, prepare, access, administer, and review medication at the key handoffs.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Responsibilities are assigned to appropriate clinical, nursing, pharmacy, or supervisory roles.","grader_hint":"Role clarity must be operational enough for a covering clinician or new nurse to act."},{"rubric_item_id":"r08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design includes a reliable patient-identity and medication-order verification before administration.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The document requires matching the patient, medication, order, route or timing, and relevant safety checks.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent clinically sound verification language; do not require a particular software workflow."},{"rubric_item_id":"r09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design addresses storage that is secure while avoiding the wall-mounted cabinet arrangement excluded by the ligature-risk context.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The selected physical arrangement is described with access control and environmental suitability.","grader_hint":"A proposal that simply reinstates wall-mounted cabinets fails this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"r10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Access to medication is limited to authorized personnel and is auditable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The document describes authorization, access recording or equivalent accountability, and response to unapproved access.","grader_hint":"Look for a control that would allow investigation of who accessed medication and when."},{"rubric_item_id":"r11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Controlled medications receive stronger custody and reconciliation controls than ordinary stock.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The document covers custody, count or reconciliation, discrepancy handling, and authorized witnessing where applicable.","grader_hint":"A generic statement that all drugs are locked is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"r12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Refrigerated medications are stored and monitored in a manner that protects product integrity and limits unauthorized access.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The design identifies temperature control or monitoring, access restriction, and action when conditions fall outside limits.","grader_hint":"Award for an operational response, not merely the word 'refrigerated'."},{"rubric_item_id":"r13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The delivery handoff from Alderwick Solutions is controlled, checked, and reconciled to what was expected.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The document specifies receipt verification, discrepancy recording, secure transfer, and responsible roles.","grader_hint":"The handoff must address both twice-daily timing and missing or unexpected items."},{"rubric_item_id":"r14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design explains how pharmacist review is obtained or documented when it cannot occur before the clinical need.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The workflow preserves retrospective or delayed review without treating it as permission to bypass clinical authorization.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit relationship between immediate need and later pharmacist review."},{"rubric_item_id":"r15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Medication changes, discontinuations, transfers, and returned or unused doses are reconciled so obsolete supply is not left active.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The document addresses status changes and physical or electronic disposition.","grader_hint":"Award when the design closes the loop rather than only describing new orders."},{"rubric_item_id":"r16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document defines a response to a medication discrepancy, including immediate safety action, notification, investigation, and recordkeeping.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"A discrepancy escalation pathway is present and sequenced sensibly.","grader_hint":"A statement to 'report discrepancies' without a safe immediate response is incomplete."},{"rubric_item_id":"r17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design covers omitted, refused, delayed, or unavailable doses and distinguishes clinical assessment from administrative closure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The workflow requires recording the event and obtaining appropriate clinical follow-up.","grader_hint":"Check that a missed or refused dose is not silently erased or automatically reissued."},{"rubric_item_id":"r18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Urgent changes in a patient's medication regimen are communicated across nursing, prescriber, pharmacy, and handoff points.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The document identifies the relevant recipients and the record that carries the change.","grader_hint":"Award only when communication is tied to an operational handoff."},{"rubric_item_id":"r19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design includes an explicit escalation path for a medication-related clinical emergency or adverse reaction.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Immediate patient safety response, clinician notification, documentation, and follow-up are addressed.","grader_hint":"Do not require a specific emergency number; assess whether the escalation is usable."},{"rubric_item_id":"r20","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The teaching section explicitly tells staff not to borrow, share, or carry forward another patient's medication.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"A direct prohibition or equivalent unambiguous instruction appears.","grader_hint":"The misuse must be actively discouraged, not merely described as a risk."},{"rubric_item_id":"r21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The teaching section explains why emergency or override supply must not be used for convenience or routine stock substitution.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The document connects convenience use to patient-specific safety, inventory accountability, or diversion risk.","grader_hint":"Look for both the prohibition and its operational reason."},{"rubric_item_id":"r22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design places a boundary around emergency or override access so it is exceptional, authorized, documented, and reconciled.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The document defines triggering conditions and controls before and after use.","grader_hint":"This is distinct from merely having an emergency pathway for first doses or PRNs."},{"rubric_item_id":"r23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document addresses patient privacy and minimizes unnecessary disclosure in medication records and handoffs.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The design limits medication information to those who need it and identifies appropriate record handling.","grader_hint":"Assess practical minimization rather than demanding a particular privacy citation."},{"rubric_item_id":"r24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The selected design includes a workable shift-change or coverage handoff for pending orders, supply gaps, PRNs, and unresolved discrepancies.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The handoff content and responsible owner are specified.","grader_hint":"A general call for communication is not enough."},{"rubric_item_id":"r25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies at least two plausible alternative designs and explains why each is unsuitable under this unit's constraints.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternatives are named or clearly described and rejected using physical, clinical, pharmacy, or diversion evidence.","grader_hint":"The alternatives must be genuinely plausible, not straw men."},{"rubric_item_id":"r26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The reasoning recognizes the conflict between rapid first-dose or PRN access and diversion control and shows how the chosen design balances them.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The tradeoff is explicitly analyzed and resolved in the recommendation.","grader_hint":"Do not award for listing the two concerns without explaining the balancing choice."},{"rubric_item_id":"r27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design is consistent with the applicable requirements in the Cavendish Authority source, including any source-specific emergency, storage, documentation, or review requirements it invokes.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Claims about requirements can be traced to the supplied standard and are not contradicted by it.","grader_hint":"Compare every cited or paraphrased rule to the reference PDF; do not reward invented requirements."},{"rubric_item_id":"r28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document defines the records needed to demonstrate medication custody, administration, exceptions, and follow-up.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The record set or required fields are described sufficiently for an audit trail.","grader_hint":"Assess coverage of custody, administration, exceptions, and follow-up as separate record purposes."},{"rubric_item_id":"r29","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A new nurse or covering prescriber could use the document without asking Cordelia to resolve basic ownership, access, escalation, or handoff questions.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reviewer assesses clarity, role assignment, decision boundaries, and practical usability.","grader_hint":"Judge the artifact as an implementation briefing, not as an academic discussion."},{"rubric_item_id":"r30","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document teaches the intended behavior clearly enough to change practice, rather than merely describing risks or reproducing regulatory language.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reviewer assesses whether the teaching section gives memorable, actionable boundaries and explains the reason for them.","grader_hint":"A technically accurate but non-instructional summary should not receive full credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"r31","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered document is no more than six pages.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX page count is six or fewer.","grader_hint":"Render with standard office settings and count pages; do not infer from source text length."},{"rubric_item_id":"r32","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document ends with the chosen design and its rationale, not with unresolved next steps or an unranked action list.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The final substantive section or closing paragraph restates the decision and why it fits the constraints.","grader_hint":"A short implementation implication is acceptable, but the ending must be a decision rather than an open assignment."},{"rubric_item_id":"r33","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required deliverable is supplied as `bellwether_medication_management_design.docx`.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The file exists with the exact required filename and DOCX format.","grader_hint":"Check the top-level working directory and file extension."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is an enterprise behavioral-health medication-management design for a new adult inpatient unit. The audience is the project lead and implementation team, who need a clinically credible design that can be translated into construction, staffing, pharmacy, and governance decisions.","difficulty_sources":["competing medication-access, ligature-risk, pharmacist-review, and diversion-control requirements","reconciliation of an operational source document with clinical medication-management practice","selection and justification of one design rather than presenting an unranked list of options","handling an informal promise that conflicts with safe professional practice","active prevention of a common unsafe medication-handling misuse"],"authoring_plan":{"reference_file":{"filename":"cavendish_medication_governance_standard.pdf","content":["Create one concise fictional Cavendish Authority standard dated before the Bellwether Ridge spring opening and applicable to adult behavioral-health inpatient units.","State that medication storage must be secured from patients and unauthorized staff, must account for ligature-risk and tamper-resistant physical design, and must not depend on wall-mounted cabinets where the physical-plant assessment excludes them.","Define that contracted pharmacy deliveries require a documented receiving handoff, verification against expected patient-specific and contingency items, secure placement, and discrepancy escalation.","Require pharmacist review of medication orders within the standard review window, while allowing a clinically authorized emergency or first-dose pathway when waiting for the next delivery or review would create an unsafe delay; require retrospective pharmacist review and documentation.","Require patient-specific labeling and verification before administration, and prohibit use of another patient's medication for a different patient.","Require controlled-medication custody, shift or access reconciliation, witnessed discrepancy handling, and prompt escalation.","Require temperature monitoring and corrective action for refrigerated medication storage.","Require documentation of administrations, refusals, omissions, PRN indication and response, emergency access, returns or wastage, medication changes, and unresolved issues at handoff.","Require privacy-minimizing access to medication information and disclosure only to people involved in care, operations, safety, or authorized oversight.","State that emergency or override stock is for defined urgent clinical need only, is not routine convenience stock, and must be inventoried and reconciled after use.","Include no private codes, invented medication names, patient identifiers, or numerical quantities that the solver would have to guess."]},"gold_solution":{"design_intent":"The reference DOCX should choose a centralized, secure, access-controlled medication service point or mobile secured medication solution that is compatible with the ligature-risk review, supported by twice-daily pharmacy delivery, and paired with narrowly bounded emergency access. It should not rely on wall-mounted cabinets or an unrestricted ward stock room.","required_reasoning":["Reject an open or broadly stocked unit medication room because it increases diversion and patient-access risk and does not solve the physical-plant concern.","Reject a wall-mounted automated cabinet model because the stated ligature-risk construction constraint rules it out.","Reject a design that waits for pharmacist review or the next delivery for every first dose and PRN because it conflicts with timely clinical care.","Reject a large unrestricted override stock because it conflicts with diversion controls and makes routine convenience use more likely."],"professional_content":["Explain intake and verification of orders, including identity, allergies or relevant clinical checks, authorization, and patient-specific labeling.","Describe twice-daily delivery receiving, verification, secure transfer, and missing-item escalation.","Provide a controlled emergency pathway for first doses and urgent PRNs with authorization, limited access, administration documentation, and later pharmacy review.","Describe separate controls for controlled drugs and refrigerated drugs.","Include shift handoff, medication changes, refusals, omissions, returns, recalls, discrepancies, adverse reactions, and urgent escalation.","Include a direct teaching message: never borrow, share, relabel, carry forward, or administer another patient's medication; never use emergency stock merely to avoid a pharmacy request or ordinary supply process.","Minimize patient-identifying details and avoid unsupported claims about legal authority or insurance."],"format_intent":"Keep the deliverable within six rendered pages, with clear headings, a compact workflow or responsibility table if useful, an alternatives-and-tradeoffs section, and a final decision statement."},"derivation":"Build the reference solution from the source rules and the professional criteria in the rubric, then have reviewers confirm that every source-specific claim is traceable and that the design is operationally usable without exposing the authoring plan to the solver."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"cavendish_medication_governance_standard","filename":"cavendish_medication_governance_standard.pdf","purpose":"Governing source for Cavendish Authority medication storage, emergency supply, pharmacist review, controlled-drug reconciliation, documentation, escalation, and behavioral-health safety requirements.","required":true,"discovery":"Read this file before designing the medication-management package; it is the sole supplied source and should be treated as the applicable standard for the Bellwether Ridge unit."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"bellwether_medication_management_design","filename":"bellwether_medication_management_design.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A single instructional implementation briefing specifying and defending the medication-management design for Bellwether Ridge Behavioral Hospital's new 24-bed adult inpatient unit.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be written as one coherent design, not a menu of equally recommended options.","Must be no more than six pages when rendered with ordinary document settings.","Must address clinical workflow, physical plant, pharmacy delivery timing, access safeguards, documentation, escalation, and implementation responsibilities.","Must include the alternatives ruled out and the reason each is unsuitable for this unit.","Must include an explicit teaching section discouraging borrowing, sharing, carrying forward, or convenience use of another patient's or emergency medication.","Must not invent medication names, patient identifiers, quantities, or unsupported claims about the governing standard.","Must end with the selected design and its rationale rather than unresolved next steps."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"document_information","work_product":"clinical_administrative_package","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","sector":"healthcare_social_assistance","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":6.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Have a behavioral-health medication-management subject-matter reviewer and an independent clinical-document usability reviewer assess the reference solution against the supplied Cavendish Authority standard. Resolve disagreements by requiring traceability for source-specific claims and practical defensibility for professional-practice criteria.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The supplied standard is fictional and self-contained, so the reference solution should not rely on live jurisdictional research.","Several clinically defensible implementations may satisfy the design criteria if they preserve the source requirements and make one coherent choice.","Page count may vary slightly across office renderers; use a standard offline DOCX renderer for the authoritative check."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:49:53+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:49:53+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:49:53+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:49:53+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":false,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"instruction 与 rubric criterion 存在 8-gram 逐字重叠: ['r32']","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:49:53+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/57acf27f31f7df11","declared_deliverables":["bellwether_medication_management_design.docx"]};
